[Music]  the u.n estimates more than one and a  half million people have fled ukraine  since russia's invasion of that country  the european union says as many as 5  million ukrainians could eventually flee  if the fighting continues  this is the fastest growing refugee  crisis in europe since world war ii  david miliband is president and ceo of  the international rescue committee which  oversees relief operations in more than  40 countries affected by the war david  good morning to you  i'm wondering your thoughts as you said  good morning what are your thoughts as  you see this sea of humanity crossing  the borders what stands out to you what  concerns you the most at this time  i think the biggest impression is it  could be me or you these people are  teachers they're journalists they're  charity workers they're business people  they're housewives so refugees who are  fleeing inside ukraine and then crossing  the border are just people what concerns  me most is two things first of all on  the european side of the border people  are obviously safe but there needs to be  proper organization so that the systems  are not overwhelmed i think the european  union has made a very good start in that  inside ukraine i'm desperately fearful  about the people who are trapped in  besieged cities people without water  heating and in some cases healthcare  which is an area where the international  rescue committee specializes so we've  got real fear for people's lives inside  the country and we've got real job to  make sure that people are safe across  the border yeah the situation david does  seem so desperate and heartbreaking tony  decopal who's there as you know told the  story yesterday that a woman told him  of reports of women being raped  by russian soldiers have you heard  stories like that as well  i've certainly read that in the media  we've also got unaccompanied children  and we've got the terrible sight of  civilians trying to get out of besieged  cities being targeted themselves that is  a war crime there's no other way to  describe it david nate burleson here now  the european union is allowing ukrainian  refugees to stay and work for up to  three years what do you make of that  that's good news nate good to have that  question the european union has really  stepped up in the past the 27 countries  of the european union have been divided  on refugee questions today they're  united all 27 countries guaranteeing not  just the right to entry not just the  right to work but the right for every  kid to go to school every adult to  access any social services they need  what organizations like mine do like the  international rescue community we're  there in poland with a team that's going  to branch out across europe we already  have services in greece and in germany  we're really going to provide our  expertise to make sure that the kids are  properly brought into the local  schooling system recognizing the trauma  that they've been through that there's  labor market help for employment for  adults never forgetting that the work we  do across the border into ukraine and  across conflict lines literally keeps  people alive that's why we focus on  health care for example why we focus on  the protection of kids david how does  this treatment compare to refugees from  syria and afghanistan  in the case of the european union we've  got unity now where there was disunity  before we've got welcome now where there  was a very divided response  before in the case of the syrian crisis  it took three months for a million  people to flee syria in the case of  ukraine it's taken a week and we're now  up to one and a half million and and why  do you think that is a lot of people are  starting to say listen when people were  coming here from el salvador honduras  they were they were not  welcome they were not really well they  were not received very well  uh when you talk about syria and  afghanistan too  what do you think is the difference here  well it's simple there's more fear of  incorporating integrating people from  different races and different religions  into national life let's be honest about  it now what we know from the history of  this country the international rescue  committee was founded by albert einstein  we've worked here for 80 years and  around the world for 80 years as well  what we know  is that in the end people fleeing for  their freedom and for their lives  they're desperate to have a second  chance and the countries that they adopt  as their new homes they become patriotic  and productive citizens because of the  chance that it's given them yeah i think  people need to be reminded that you know  we had time and time you look at people  coming from haiti people coming as i  said el salvador honduras they were not  treated this well and i think we all  want the ukrainian people to be helped  everybody agrees with that but there is  enough pain to go around here thank you  so much for your time thank you david  thank you

